# Effective writing

Write to make useful work durable, not to turn every session into documentation. A good record lets someone find, understand, evaluate, and continue a subject after the transcript and your context are gone. The structure serves creation; it is not a substitute for it.

## Create when creation is the work

Search before creating when the subject may already exist. Extending or connecting an existing artifact usually preserves a more coherent world than describing the same thing again. But do not treat creation as inherently suspect. When a new proposal, decision, model, plan, prototype, or other artifact is the deliverable, will inform later work, or needs to survive this session, make it readily and give it a durable identity.

The right granularity is the unit a future reader will want to retrieve or change on its own. A whole session compressed into one record hides the distinct decisions and artifacts inside it. A record for every trivial step buries the outcome beneath process. Prefer one meaningful subject per record, with supporting material connected where it can be found.

Early proposals can be both durable and disposable. Preserve enough context to evaluate them, label their maturity honestly, and make them cheap to adapt or supersede. Durability does not mean pretending a draft is settled.

## Make it findable from more than one direction

Name the subject in the plain terms a later reader is likely to use. Avoid titles that depend on the current conversation, chronology, or private shorthand. A future reader should be able to distinguish the artifact in a list before opening it.

Names are only one way into the world. Choose the record type by what the thing is, and use an honest kind for the more specific shape. Place it in the canonical browse home where the relevant people will look. Use links for dependencies, provenance, implementation, supersession, and other relationships rather than trying to make containment express every association. Add governed facets when they answer real future filters or workflow questions, resolving their current vocabulary instead of guessing.

Before choosing the immutable `type` and its required type-relative `kind`, call `schema_read.preview_record_shape` with the current candidate. A type-only preview supplies the closed type meanings, governed kinds, base facets, and guaranteed relationship meanings; add the nonblank kind to receive its live resolution, exact matches under other types, effective facet shape, provenance, and the consequences of an honest novel kind. Use those deterministic facts before interpreting the name, body, or intent.

The preview is advisory and carries no commit token. `create_record` remains directly callable and independently revalidates the live write. A preview does not establish future permission, write admission, target validity, or state after its snapshot. It assesses only the fields its live operation contract accepts and never infers replacement values or links. If the preview is unavailable or stale, say so and continue when creation is still the right work rather than treating missing advice as an authorization failure.

Keep interpretation visibly separate from governed facts. If a name, body, or intent suggests a type, kind, facet, or link, label that conclusion as interpretation and carry the evidence and uncertainty that support it. Never present a heuristic suggestion as governed, silently rewrite the candidate, or claim a rewrite is lossless merely because one facet overlaps part of the meaning. For example, `ci-build-cost` does not collapse to an `area` facet without preserving its distinct retrieval meaning, and an honest `essay-draft` must not lose either genre or maturity. A novel kind remains available when it is the truthful subtype; report its governed-dispatch consequences instead of inventing a canonical replacement.

`record-types`, `placement`, `links`, and `facets-and-vocabularies` carry the exact contracts. Use their mechanics in service of discoverability. Do not invent elaborate structure before the work demonstrates a need for it, and prefer an honest staging location to confident misfiling.

## Put the reasoning with the outcome

The body should let a capable reader understand why the artifact exists and how to assess it. State the subject and purpose without relying on the session. Preserve the consequential options, evidence, trade-offs, and assumptions behind a decision. Name what remains open and what would cause the conclusion to be revisited.

This is not an invitation to store a transcript of your reasoning. Capture the part another reader needs to continue or re-evaluate the work. Routine tool use, abandoned wording, and intermediate mechanics rarely belong. A concise rationale with its decisive evidence is more useful than a long chronology of how you arrived there.

When a write asks for `reason`, explain why the change is being made rather than repeating its title, body, or field values. Preserve the alternatives or objections that mattered, why this course won, and what a later reader would need to reconsider it. If the answer is obvious, say what made it obvious. The record already carries what changed; `reason` carries the consequential reasoning that would otherwise be lost.

Keep authorship clear. Separate:

- facts and source material;
- the user's stated goals, preferences, and decisions;
- another contributor's proposal or conclusion;
- your own inference, recommendation, or creation.

You may originate strong ideas. Attribute them to yourself as agent judgement rather than hiding them or turning them into the user's view. When you extend another contributor's work, preserve what came from them and make your addition visible. Shared credentials do not make shared authorship.

## Put content where its provenance belongs

Authored knowledge—briefs, decisions, analyses, plans, and write-ups—belongs in the body of a durable record, with its own record when it is a substantial subject. Captured or derived source material—transcripts, fetched pages, raw traces, snapshots, and raw generated output used as provenance—belongs as an attachment rather than the authored conclusion. A generated result intended to be used or evaluated as the outcome should retain an appropriate first-class record or artifact identity. The distinction is what the content is doing, not merely how large it is. `attachment` carries the exact storage and reading contract.

When an external artifact will matter after this session or to another contributor, leave a durable, openable path to it and enough context to understand what it is, why it matters, and what state it is in. Do not replace the actual deliverable with a record describing a hypothetical one.

## Correct forward

When the world changes or a conclusion proves wrong, preserve the fact that it changed. Update the current record honestly, record the new reasoning that matters, and use an explicit supersession relationship when one durable position replaces another. History should let the next reader distinguish what was believed then from what governs now.

Correction-forward discipline should increase willingness to contribute a reasoned draft; it should not license careless writing. Verify consequential facts, make uncertainty visible, and avoid rewriting the past to make the present look inevitable.

## Leave the work ready to continue

Before finishing, ask whether someone outside this session can find the artifact, tell what it is, see where its claims came from, distinguish settled from provisional material, and know the next meaningful decision. Add only what closes a real gap in that hand-off.

The user should receive the artifact and the decisions it creates, not a report about record hygiene. When durable structure works well, it recedes beneath useful work that another person or agent can pick up without re-briefing.
